{
 "summary": "Plugs Bitcoin Core's hardware-optimized SHA256 compression function into libsecp256k1 contexts. It retires the ECC_Context RAII lifecycle class in favor of thread-safe, on-first-use static contexts for signing and public operations, and replaces uses of secp256k1_context_static across pubkey, MuSig2, and BIP352 code with these accelerated contexts.",
 "problem": "libsecp256k1 relies on its internal portable SHA256 implementation by default, leaving hardware crypto acceleration (SHA-NI, ARMv8, SSE4) unused during signing and internal hashing. In addition, libsecp256k1's static context cannot accept a custom compression function, and the previous ECC_Context pattern required boilerplate RAII lifecycle management throughout node binaries, tools, benchmarks, and tests.",
 "evidence": [
  "Author's benchmarks on ARM64 using arm_shani report a 13% reduction in ECDSASign latency (79.2 µs to 69.3 µs) and a 7% reduction in SchnorrSign latency (39.9 µs to 37.1 µs), with smaller improvements in EllSwiftCreate (-3%) and BIP324_ECDH (-1%)."
 ],
